A||N||4||Taijiguy||Roller lifter info pleeze.....||||5||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||05-11-2000||11:44 PM||||OK, so at the risk of having my Enthusiasts card taken away, and enduring all kinds of sideways looks, here's my query; what's the difference in the profile/grind of a roller cam, versus a hydraulic or mechanincal cam. And why can't you use roller lifters on a hydraulic cam? The cam is ground to run on the center of the lifter, whic is where the roller is, so what would be the problem? Not being from the "that's just the way it's done" school, I'm curious why this isn't done. <P>Thanks<P>-John||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierosound||05-12-2000||12:30 AM||||The lobe profile of a roller cam has much steeper ramp profiles to open and close the valves much faster, which is why they generally are considerd to be a "higher performance" cam.  This is possible because of the smaller contact surface of the roller lifter where it contacts the cam, and the lower friction provided by the fact that the roller "rolls".  <P>A regular hydraulic lifter has a broad flat surface in comparison, and the standard cam is ground differently to match the wear characteristics with this profile.  This lifter "drags" across the cam's lobe profile, with only engine oil to reduce friction and wear.  Using this lifter with a roller cam would destroy the camshaft in short order as the cam lobe profile and lifter "won't match".<P>See the website below to camshaft basics. <A HREF="http://www.amotion.com/tech/camtalk.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.amotion.com/tech/camtalk.html</A> <P>||||reg||5
Z||000002||Taijiguy||05-12-2000||12:42 AM||||I wasn't wondering about using a hydraulic lifter on a roller cam. (YIKES!) I wanted know about using roller lifters on a hydraulic cam. I know rollers are quieter and run easier. So why not use the lifters on a cam with a lower profile (hydraulic) to gain those benefits?||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-12-2000||10:25 AM||||There is more than a valve timing diferance in the way the cams are ground.<P>You cannot put flat lifters on a roller cam, nor can you put rollers on a cam for flat lifters. (most rollers are hydraulic. Don't class them as hydraulic or rollers. class them as flat or rollers.)<P>The roller lifter does not work the same as a flat one. The contact point/angle of a roller lifter actually moves as it encounters diferant angles on the cam. It's hard to explain but it does. The cam is ground with this factor figured in.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||05-12-2000||12:53 PM||||Fierosound and ogre hit the nail on the head.<P>If you want to go roller, buy a the cam and lifters as a set.  Forget trying to create your own combo.  Cam makers spend a lot of time and research.  Benefit from it.  Your don't have the R&D budget to beat them.||||reg||1
